  "provenance": {
    "round": "sounds-r2",
    "module": "wave-pad.ts",
    "note": "Two spectra and a crossfade between them, which is the linear interpolation a wavetable synthesiser actually computes.",
    "notes": {
      "hollow": "The first frame: odd harmonics at 1/n with the third and fifth pulled down, which is a closed pipe and reads dark and woody.",
      "humpAt": "The second: every harmonic at 1/sqrt(n) with a hump five partials up, which is what makes a spectrum read as a mouth rather than as a filter.",
      "layers": "`[octave, level, pan]`. The morph is between two *waves*, so the two oscillators of a pair may not be detuned — that would be a flanger — and the width is a second pair an octave up instead."
    }
  }
