 "note": "The hats' transition vocabulary (his word 10-16 on house seed 35: 'space is huge, so congas fill in well that upper mid freq, that said we could explore a bit more interesting hi-hat patterns in sub-segments transitions'; standing: 'tracks alive: the small things, fills, breaks, variations every few bars'). At the bar into each of the arrangement's sub-segments and out of it (src/phrasing.ts: a drop-out bar, a filtered, a half-time or a breakdown-lite phrase), into and out of each of the house's own hat variations, and where a noise transition was refused (recipes/arrangement/seams.json: the drum work in its place), the hats play one of these rows: a short figure on their own lanes, drawn per transition off a stream of its own, leaned by the birds, never the same move twice in a row within a theme. A row names lanes, steps and levels, never a voice: every hit is a copy of a hit the theme's own hats play (src/subsections.ts), so no row adds an instrument. Since round hat-scene (his word 10-16: 'voices give it a main gravity that hats can work of on segments while adding their accents for vectors of track change upward/downward/seam on similar segment parts for liveliness') each transition carries the vector the voices make across its seam (recipes/arrangement/hat-scene.json `vector`: their energy two bars before against two from the line, the hats left out), and a row's `vectors` lean it: lifts, building rolls and triplets into more; thinning, chokes, gaps and the roll that thins into less; on a level seam a small answer to the figure. Where the bar's voices are busy the busy rows are kept out, as they are out of the hand player's room. Since round hat-seams a span's way out that a build holds is led into by the hats from the bar before the build (the seam's upward vector; never where the build is the hats' own roll), the answer answers the bass's line where the figure rests and the bass carries the bar, and an answer under a call still sounding (legato keys ringing into each other, a held bass) reads through the sustain. Every number is a starting point for listening.",
 "units": {
  "says": "a pattern is sixteenths right-aligned to the bar's end (one per window length, by beats); a digit is a hit at that many tenths of the lane's own loudest hit in the bar, `o` a ghost (its quietest at 0.3), a row's own letters as written: `level` its tenths as a share, `shift` sixteenths off its step (a thirty-second's flam half of one, a triplet a third), `chokeAfter` its ring cut that many sixteenths after it. A move stays under the groove (0.7 of the lane's loudest hit at most), but a `full` row, an accent into the line, reaches the lane's own level on its last or main hit, so the accent never reads as a dip. The lanes: `closed`, the closed hats alone; `open`, the open hat (copied from the theme's own, whichever bar it is in); `offbeat`, both. `clear`: the lane's own hits out from the window's start (a row that only clears takes `take` hits at least, or it is never drawn there). A row is drawn only where it changes the hats: a move that would add nothing never spends the transition. `busy`: a row of many small hits, kept out of the hand player's room. `needs`: `open`, the theme plays an open hat of its own voice whose ring to -10 dB is `open.ring` seconds at least (a tight hat in the open slot has no tail to choke, lift or wash); `talk`, the hand part strikes inside the window (the gap answers it); `call`, the theme's own figure (the keys, the stabs, the lead) strikes in the bar (a part a track recipe or a pin lays is never read). `vectors`: the row's weight by the vector of change across the seam, `up` into more, `down` into less, `level` (1 where a row names none; never drawn at nought, and a row with a nought is drawn only where the seam's vector is read: a theme whose parts a track recipe lays reads none). `keeps`: the row's weight by the vector where the call keeps playing across the seam (it strikes in the move's bar and in the line's: the figure, or the bass where the figure rests, so a bass line carried into a drop-out or a filtered phrase answers there; his ear 10-17, 'hats sound good'), in place of `vectors` there. `answer`: call and response, the closed hats over the window on their offbeats at `time` but where the figure strikes, and an answer to each of its strikes at `hit`, in its gap before the next strike: on the first sixteenth after it where no note of the figure sounds (every one stopped `clear` of a sixteenth before it at least: a strum's late voices, the hats' own swing and humanising), or where it rings on, on the next offbeat half a beat after it at least, never on a kick; `answer.ring`: where the call still sounds at the answer, the answer on the open hat at `open`, choked, where the theme's own rings (`open.ring`) and the span keeps its tops, else on the closed hats at `hit` (it may reach the lane's own level: the response read through the sustain, never under the strike) with a ghost at `after` on the sixteenth after it (the and-a, so the answer is never just the groove's own offbeat), and the window's other offbeats at `time`. The call is the figure's strikes, or the bass's where the figure rests and the bass carries the bar (recipes/arrangement/hat-scene.json `call`)",
